<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/06/image2-1791286747112-854c166f7c.png" alt="Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p2">Yesterday, Apple announced <a href="https://developer.apple.com/news/?id=kkphp5qo" rel="noopener noreferrer">on its developer blog</a> that it has begun taking App Store submissions for iPhone Duo apps. The new foldable phone will be in customers’ hands in just over two weeks on October 23 and with Xcode 27.1, developers can recompile existing apps and build new ones.</p>
<p id="p3">To prepare for the launch, Apple has provided links to <a href="https://developer.apple.com/app-store/asset-best-practices/" rel="noopener noreferrer">design</a> and <a href="https://developer.apple.com/help/app-store-connect/manage-app-information/manage-your-app-store-assets" rel="noopener noreferrer">App Store asset</a> guidance, as well as <a href="https://developer.apple.com/app-store/submitting/" rel="noopener noreferrer">submitting apps to App Store Connect</a>.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/06/image1-1791286700840-9764e23aca.png" alt="Federico's Frames CLI."><p class="image-caption">Federico’s Frames CLI.</p></div>
<p id="p5">Also, if you’re a developer, don’t forget to check out Federico’s Frames CLI 1.5.0, which includes experimental support for the iPhone Duo. The feedback from developers has been that it saves a lot of time when preparing screenshots for the App Store. The Frames CLI <a href="https://github.com/viticci/frames-cli" rel="noopener noreferrer">is available on GitHub</a> and there’s <a href="https://www.macstories.net/notes/frames-cli-1-5-0-now-with-iphone-18-pro-and-experimental-iphone-duo-support/" rel="noopener noreferrer">more information on MacStories here</a>.</p>
